<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Frigidus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/394">394</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Frigidus was fought on 5–6 September 394 by the river Frigidus, probably the Vipava in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>. The eastern emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> defeated the western usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a> and his Frankish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a>, helped by a strong wind, the bora, blowing in his soldiers' favour. The victory reunited the empire for the last time and was celebrated as a triumph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> over a pagan revival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-frigidus.jpg" alt="Battle of the Frigidus" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Weikhard von Valvasor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janez_Vajkard_Valvasor_-_Bitka_med_Teodozijem_in_Evgenijem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 15 May 392 the western emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-ii">Valentinian II</a> was found dead at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. His magister militum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a>, told Theodosius, the eastern emperor and Valentinian's brother-in-law, that the young emperor had killed himself, but Theodosius came to believe he had been murdered, partly because his wife Galla was convinced that her brother had died by treachery. On 22 August Arbogast raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a>, a respected scholar of rhetoric and the western court's magister scrinii, to the western throne. Eugenius removed most of the high officers Theodosius had left in the West, and when western ambassadors came to Constantinople Theodosius gave them presents and vague promises. In January 393 he declared his eight-year-old son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> western augustus and resolved to invade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Over the next year and a half <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stilicho">Stilicho</a> and Timasius restored discipline and numbers to the eastern army, which had declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">Battle of Adrianople</a>. The army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in May 394 with over 20,000 Visigoth federates and forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and its commanders included Alaric and Bacurius the Iberian. Arbogast, hoping to keep his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Alemanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallo-romans">Gallo-Romans</a> and Gothic auxiliaries united to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, left the Alpine passes unguarded. The eastern army descended towards the Frigidus, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, and found the western camp in the first days of September.</p><p>On 5 September Theodosius attacked at once with little reconnaissance, sending his Gothic allies in first, perhaps to thin their ranks. Some 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> are reported to have died, and Bacurius was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a> sent men to close the passes behind Theodosius, but they proved ready to desert to him. On 6 September a fierce wind, apparently the bora, blew down the valley, and Arbogast's lines broke. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a> was captured and beheaded. Arbogast fled into the mountains and killed himself after a few days. The site is uncertain: it is usually placed in the Vipava Valley, with the Frigidus as the Vipava, and recent research suggests a place between Col and Sanabor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Church historians attributed the victory to divine intervention, and Rufinus compared its importance to Constantine's victory at the Milvian Bridge. Alan Cameron argues that the idea that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenius">Eugenius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arbogast">Arbogast</a> were pagans was created to justify the campaign, and Eugenius was not a pagan. The earliest source for the wind, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a>, says it blew before the battle and demoralised the enemy, and the poet Claudian moved it to the decisive moment. A contemporary historian said Theodosius won two battles, one against Eugenius and one against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, who suffered most. He died four months later, leaving his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honorius">Honorius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a>. The losses weakened the western legions and increased reliance on foederati.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Frigidus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
